PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Current leaning: D. CAGGACCGGTTTCAGATGCG(CGG) is a full 20-nt spCas9 protospacer with canonical CGG PAM. Its reverse complement maps to the second exon, around the plus-strand sequence CGCATCTGAAACCGGTCCTG within “…GCTTCCGCGCATCTGAAACCGGTCCTGTGG…”. Thus it targets exon 2 on the reverse strand. Colleagues 1–3 support D and correctly emphasize exon location, full length, and valid PAM.

Main disagreement: colleagues 4–5 favor K (CTGCTCTACCCAATCGCCA(TGG)), and 6–7 favor E (GCATCTGAAACCGGTCCTG(TGG)). Both K and E lie in exon 2, but by count each appears to be 19 nt rather than the standard 20-nt protospacer. E overlaps the same locus as D but is shifted/truncated: the expected 20-nt plus-strand target would be CGCATCTGAAACCGGTCCTG(TGG), while E omits the 5′ C. K also appears 19 nt and its “low-GC seed” rationale is debatable; its overall GC content is not especially low. D’s CGG PAM may reduce off-targets compared with TGG PAMs, though CGG is still canonical. D contains a CCGG motif, but that alone is not disqualifying.

Other choices: N is in the first exon; H/J are intronic/repetitive/low-complexity; L/O are too short or use noncanonical PAMs; several others are not clearly in exon 2 or lack valid NGG PAM. Unresolved: whether 19-nt guide choices E/K would still be acceptable in practice. For best heuristic without whole-genome data, D remains strongest; E could be fallback only if 19-nt guides are tolerated.}

